1. Inspect CSV structure: Read offline_data/regional_sales.csv: list all column names, total row count, date range (min/max of the month column), unique regions, unique product lines, and print first 5 rows as sample → db_agent
2. Compute 24-month rolling mean & anomaly multiples: Using the CSV, for each (region × product_line) compute the 24-month trailing mean of monthly sales volume, then compute the ratio = current_month_sales / 24-month_mean for every month. Save the full result table to a CSV file (e.g. offline_data/anomaly_multiples.csv) → code_agent
3. Identify top-3 anomalies & fetch return rates: From the anomaly CSV, select the top-3 (region, product_line, month) by highest multiple. For each, also retrieve the corresponding return_rate from the original CSV. Save a small summary CSV (top3_anomalies.csv) with columns: region, product_line, month, sales_volume, mean_24m, multiple, return_rate → code_agent
4. Assess real demand vs channel stuffing: For each of the top-3 anomalies, compute supporting signals from the CSV: (a) return_rate level, (b) whether the spike is a single-month spike or sustained (check next 1-2 months' sales vs mean), (c) compare with the same product_line in other regions in the same month. Save a signals CSV (top3_signals.csv) → code_agent
5. Write Markdown report with embedded data: Write a comprehensive Markdown report to offline_data/regional_sales_anomaly_report.md. Requirements: (1) H1 title; (2) at least one data table showing the top-3 anomalies with all key numbers; (3) every key number annotated with source in the format '（来源：offline_data/regional_sales.csv 的 <列名>，<聚合口径与样本行数>）' or '（来源：由前述两行计算）'; (4) a concluding paragraph judging whether each spike is more likely real demand or channel stuffing, with reasoning; (5) all conclusions derived solely from the local CSV. Embed the top3_anomalies.csv and top3_signals.csv data as Markdown tables. → code_agent
